netgen upstream e1d71a78ab1d71d185268a9779520eb04d9be509, "no need to remove archive type
infos" (mhochsteger, 2021-09-13; first in v6.2.2105), backported to netgen 6.2.2101.

Defect in 2101: ngcore keeps the archive class register in a namespace-scope
std::unique_ptr in core/archive.cpp (type_register), and every RegisterClassForArchive
object took its class out of that register in its destructor. netgen's own build makes
ngcore a separate DLL, loaded before netgen's libraries and unloaded after them. pySMESH
links ngcore and netgen statically into _core.pyd, where netgen's registering objects
(regob in csg/brick.cpp, and others) are built before archive.cpp registers the
destructor of type_register. At process exit type_register is destroyed first, and the
later ~RegisterClassForArchive reads the freed map in Archive::IsRegistered: an access
violation during DLL_PROCESS_DETACH whenever the freed memory has been reused. Measured
before this patch: 6 of 20 processes that only imported pySMESH ended with 0xC0000005
(tests/test_mesher_netgen_process.py), stack ~RegisterClassForArchive ->
Archive::RemoveArchiveRegister -> Archive::IsRegistered -> std::map::find, from
dllmain_crt_process_detach.

Change, verbatim from upstream: RegisterClassForArchive has no destructor, and
Archive::RemoveArchiveRegister is gone (its only caller was that destructor). The register
lives until the process ends. The hunks apply at fuzz 0 with an offset of -66 lines. No
mesh changes: the register is read only by netgen's archive (serialisation) code, which
pySMESH does not call.

